      {
        "children": [
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "id": "ynlxcuetiptwd0l",
                "text": "Measuring pH"
              }
            ],
            "id": "cm0ala1jcrmrtgn",
            "type": "h1"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "The acidity of a solution is typically assessed experimentally by measurement of its pH. The pOH of a solution usually is not measured, as it is easily calculated from an experimentally determined pH value. The pH of a solution can be directly measured using a pH meter, as shown in the images below. "
              }
            ],
            "id": "q95kyfwlzzmv8f6",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "caption": [
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "strong": true,
                    "text": "(a)"
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "text": " A research-grade pH meter used in a laboratory can have a resolution of 0.001 pH units, an accuracy of ±0.002 pH units, and a cost in excess of $1000. "
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "strong": true,
                    "text": "(b)"
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "text": " A portable pH meter has lower resolution (0.01 pH units), lower accuracy (±0.2 pH units), and a far lower price tag. (Credit b: Modification of work by Jacopo Werther.)"
                  }
                ],
                "type": "p"
              }
            ],
            "children": [
              {
                "id": "geoqycmgt9dseht",
                "text": " ",
                "type": "text"
              }
            ],
            "id": "ktu030w9fmfh7yi",
            "src": "https://d2xvti2irp4c7t.cloudfront.net/media/f3/f314d6f5f157f2f6a4fd9c181fc26b79/pH_meter.jpg",
            "type": "img",
            "width": "650"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "The pH of a solution may also be visually estimated using colored indicators, as shown below."
              }
            ],
            "id": "mjm3g78c50s7alo",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "caption": [
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "strong": true,
                    "text": "(a)"
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "text": " A universal indicator assumes a different color in solutions of different pH values. Thus, it can be added to a solution to determine the pH of the solution. Each of the eight vials contains a universal indicator and 0.1 M solutions of progressively weaker acids: HCl (pH = 1), CH"
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "sub": true,
                    "text": "3"
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "text": "CO"
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "sub": true,
                    "text": "2"
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "text": "H (pH = 3), and NH"
                  },
                  {
